# THE PAIR. Two fixes only fail TOGETHER here, and each one alone passes every
# row a test of the other would write -- which is why this is one fixture and
# not two.
#
#   1. DEAD-ARM SUPPRESSION. A guarded import binds the SAME alias in both arms,
#      so the alias is a DOUBLE REGISTRATION. FindUnitOrAlias is first-wins
#      (bug-n-a-unit-alias-rebind-is-silently-ignored), so the dead try arm's
#      row is appended first and, unsuppressed, is what every later lookup
#      reaches.
#   2. THE getattr FOLD over that alias
#      (bug-n-a-module-bound-by-an-import-is-not-a-value, option 3).
#
# WHY THE PAIR AND NOT EACH HALF. The fold resolves through FindUnitOrAlias, so
# if suppression ever regresses the fold answers against the WRONG MODULE --
# silently, with a real value, in the one file whose only job is to say which
# backend is live. That is the 73312a7472fd trap one layer up: there it BOUND
# the wrong module, here it FOLDS against it. A fold test on a single-arm alias
# passes, and a suppression test without a getattr passes; neither can see it.
#
# THE GUARD IS A MODULE ABSENT UNDER BOTH CPython AND pxx, deliberately. Writing
# `import ctypes` -- which is what lekkerzeilen's seam actually writes -- would
# make CPython take the try arm and pxx the except arm, so oracle and subject
# would execute different code and the differential could not fail. The
# CONSTRUCT is what is under test, not which module is missing.
#
# Every name in the dead arm is spelled WRONG on purpose, so a regression cannot
# accidentally produce a right answer.
import nilpy_seampair as s

print("probe_live", s.probe_live())
print("probe_dead", s.probe_dead())
